  q(REST,'A Class IV cavity involves:','A front tooth’s incisal angle',['Pits on molar chewing surfaces','Only the gumline area','Only cusp tips of molars'],'D','Class IV restorations replace a proximal surface and the incisal corner of an anterior tooth.',KD),
  q(REST,'Which restorative material releases fluoride over time?','Glass ionomer',['Cast gold alloy','Porcelain','Silver amalgam'],'A','Glass ionomer bonds chemically to tooth structure and helps prevent new decay at its margins.',KD),
  q(REST,'Which material is often placed in a deep preparation to protect the pulp?','Calcium hydroxide',['Composite polish','Etching gel','Pumice paste'],'B','Calcium hydroxide liners encourage new dentin to form over a nearly exposed pulp.',KD),
  q(REST,'A Class VI cavity involves:','Incisal edges or cusp tips',['Proximal surfaces only','The gumline area only','The root surface only'],'C','Class VI was added to Black’s original system to describe wear or defects on edges and tips.',KD),
  q(HN,'The uvula hangs from which structure?','Soft palate',['Hard palate','Tongue','Pharynx'],'D','The uvula and soft palate rise during swallowing to close off the nasal cavity.',NEL),
  q(HN,'Wharton’s duct drains saliva from which gland?','Submandibular',['Parotid','Sublingual','Lacrimal gland'],'A','Wharton’s duct opens under the tongue near the lingual frenum.',NEL),
  q(HN,'Stensen’s duct drains saliva from which gland?','Parotid',['Submandibular','Sublingual','Thyroid'],'B','Stensen’s duct opens on the inner cheek near the maxillary second molar.',NEL),
  q(HN,'Which U-shaped bone in the neck supports the tongue?','Hyoid',['Vomer','Mandible','Sphenoid'],'C','The hyoid does not touch any other bone; muscles and ligaments hold it in place.',NEL),
  q(HN,'Which cranial nerve controls the muscles of facial expression?','Facial (VII)',['Trigeminal (V)','Vagus (X)','Optic (II)'],'D','A misplaced inferior alveolar injection can briefly numb the facial nerve, causing facial drooping.',NEL),
  q(RAD,'In the bisecting angle technique, the central ray is aimed perpendicular to:','An imaginary bisecting line',['The long axis of the tooth','The receptor itself','The floor of the room'],'A','The line bisects the angle between the tooth and the receptor, which cannot be placed parallel.',KD),
  q(RAD,'A herringbone pattern on a film image means the receptor was:','Placed backward',['Placed too high','Exposed twice','Overdeveloped'],'B','The lead foil pattern shows when the back of the packet faces the X-ray tube.',KD),
  q(RAD,'What does the ALARA principle stand for?','As Low As Reasonably Achievable',['All Lead Aprons Remain Available','Always Limit All Radiographs Annually','As Long As Required by Agency'],'C','ALARA means keeping radiation doses as low as possible while still getting useful images.',KD),
  q(RAD,'What is an advantage of digital dental sensors over film?','Less radiation and instant images',['More radiation and better color','No need for infection control','No need for patient consent'],'D','Digital imaging also removes chemical processing and allows images to be enlarged.',KD),
  q(RAD,'What does a collimator do on a dental X-ray unit?','Restricts the beam size',['Cools the X-ray tube','Develops the image','Measures patient dose'],'A','A smaller beam reduces the tissue exposed; rectangular collimation lowers dose further.',KD),
  q(LAB,'Zinc oxide–eugenol (ZOE) is often used as a temporary cement because it:','Soothes the pulp',['Bonds permanently','Whitens the teeth','Releases mercury'],'B','Eugenol (clove oil) has a calming effect, but it can block the setting of resin materials.',KD),
  q(LAB,'In the dental laboratory, what is a die?','A replica of one prepared tooth',['A wax bite rim for dentures','A temporary acrylic crown','A custom impression tray'],'C','Dies are made of die stone and used to build crowns and inlays with accurate margins.',KD),
  q(LAB,'Which gypsum product (Type III) is used for working casts?','Dental stone',['Model plaster','Impression plaster','Investment'],'D','Dental stone is stronger than plaster; die stone (Type IV) is stronger still.',KD),
  q(LAB,'What is investment material used for?','Surrounding a wax pattern for casting',['Taking a final impression','Cementing a finished crown','Polishing acrylic dentures'],'A','Investment withstands the high heat used when molten metal replaces the burned-out wax.',KD),
  q(LAB,'Most denture bases are made from which material?','Acrylic resin',['Feldspathic porcelain','Gold alloy','Alginate'],'B','Pink polymethyl methacrylate (PMMA) is shaped like gum tissue and holds the denture teeth.',KD)
